ASoC: lpass-macro + soundwire: qcom: let the LPASS audio path power down

The LPASS rx/tx/va/wsa macros and the Qualcomm SoundWire controller only
implement runtime PM and never request an idle after probe, and the macro
clock-gate callbacks (used by the SoundWire controllers and by the other
macros' fsgen) write macro registers without a runtime PM reference. A
macro that nothing pulls on therefore stays active forever, and one that is
allowed to idle breaks the clocks it provides. Both keep MCLK/NPL requests
to the ADSP alive.

Take a runtime PM reference in the clock-gate enable paths and drop it on
disable, let idle macros autosuspend after probe, and add system sleep
handlers to the macros and the SoundWire controller that run the runtime
suspend/resume paths for devices still runtime-active at suspend time, so
a controller kept awake for codec jack alerts still clock-stops across
system suspend.

retroforge, 7.3.1 bump: v7.3 carries the runtime-PM half itself (references in
the fsgen/swclk gate paths, autosuspend after probe) for all four macros, so
this patch is reduced to the system-sleep handlers (four macros + the SoundWire
controller).

diff --git a/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c b/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
index 55678a30c..6be7890b9 100644
--- a/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
@@ -1825,7 +1825,24 @@ static int __maybe_unused swrm_runtime_suspend(struct device *dev)
 	return 0;
 }
 
+static int __maybe_unused swrm_suspend(struct device *dev)
+{
+	if (pm_runtime_status_suspended(dev))
+		return 0;
+
+	return swrm_runtime_suspend(dev);
+}
+
+static int __maybe_unused swrm_resume(struct device *dev)
+{
+	if (pm_runtime_status_suspended(dev))
+		return 0;
+
+	return swrm_runtime_resume(dev);
+}
+
 static const struct dev_pm_ops swrm_dev_pm_ops = {
+	SET_SYSTEM_SLEEP_PM_OPS(swrm_suspend, swrm_resume)
 	SET_RUNTIME_PM_OPS(swrm_runtime_suspend, swrm_runtime_resume, NULL)
 };
 
